Reserve space before asynchronous content arrives. Give images and video intrinsic dimensions or an aspect ratio, allocate stable slots for ads and embeds, and size skeletons close to their final content. Avoid inserting content above the user's current viewport unless the user initiated it, and investigate late font swaps that change text metrics. Record layout-shift entries or use DevTools to identify the moving element, then verify CLS in field data because lab loading usually misses shifts that happen after scrolling or later in the page lifecycle. A shift caused close to a discrete user input can be excluded by the metric, but that is not a reason to leave unrelated shifts unaddressed.

```tsx caption="Intrinsic dimensions reserve the image's layout space before it loads."
<img
  src="/product.avif"
  width={640}
  height={480}
  alt="Product"
/>;
```

Font requests are commonly delayed until the render tree is built, and a font can affect both when text becomes visible and how much space it occupies. First limit the family and weights to what the page needs, use a compressed web format, and choose `font-display` deliberately. Preload only a font that is genuinely critical and ensure its `crossorigin` and URL match the CSS request; otherwise the hint can waste bandwidth. Match fallback metrics as closely as practical to reduce movement, and reserve layout space where appropriate. Check the LCP element and field CLS rather than assuming a font change helped.

```ts caption="A compressed font with an explicit display policy avoids an implicit browser choice."
@font-face {
  font-family: "App Sans";
  src: url("/app-sans.woff2") format("woff2");
  font-display: swap;
}
```

Split code when it removes work that is not needed for the current task: route-level screens, rarely used modals, editors, maps, or heavy charting libraries are common boundaries. Dynamic `import()` creates an on-demand chunk; entry and shared-dependency splitting can also prevent duplication. Splitting is harmful when critical UI waits on a chain of tiny chunks, shared dependencies are duplicated, or the loading state is a blank or unusable experience. Choose boundaries from user journeys and the dependency graph, then compare the initial waterfall, parse/execute cost, cache behavior, and interaction delay. A smaller entry bundle is not automatically a faster task if it adds late blocking requests.

```ts caption="A feature can be loaded only when the user requests it."
async function openReport() {
  const { renderReport } = await import("./report");
  renderReport();
}
```

Instrument the Core Web Vitals with a reference implementation or equivalent browser APIs, report samples as distributions, and preserve context that makes a regression actionable: route, release, device class, browser, geography, connection, and interaction or LCP element where available. Segment mobile and desktop rather than averaging them into one number, and use the 75th percentile for the standard pass discussion. Send data when the page becomes hidden as well as on unload because unload callbacks are not reliable for every lifecycle. Protect privacy and sampling quality, set alerts against a baseline, and pair field dashboards with lab traces that can explain the cause.

```ts caption="The web-vitals library exposes callbacks for collecting the three Core Web Vitals."
import { onLCP, onINP, onCLS } from "web-vitals";

for (const report of [onLCP, onINP, onCLS]) {
  report((metric) => sendToRUM(metric));
}
```

Layout thrashing is repeated forced layout caused by alternating DOM or style writes with geometry reads. After a write, the browser may need to recalculate style and layout before it can answer a read such as `getBoundingClientRect()`, so a loop can repeatedly pay that cost. Confirm it in a Performance trace rather than guessing. Batch reads before writes, calculate from cached measurements where possible, avoid querying layout inside a large loop, and schedule visual updates coherently. Also reduce the affected DOM and prefer transforms for movement when appropriate. The goal is not to ban every geometry read; it is to avoid forcing the browser to recalculate the same layout repeatedly in one frame.

```ts caption="Read geometry as a batch, then perform writes as a batch."
const heights = rows.map((row) => row.getBoundingClientRect().height);
rows.forEach((row, i) => {
  row.style.transform = `translateY(${offsets[i]}px)`;
});
```

Use `PerformanceObserver` when you need browser performance entries as they are recorded: for example LCP, layout shifts, event timings, or user timing marks. Register only the entry types you need and use buffered observation when entries may have happened before the observer was created. Treat the API as an input to a reporting design, not as the complete user experience: iframe content may be inaccessible to the top-level page, unload is not reliable for every lifecycle, and a metric such as CLS needs its defined aggregation rules rather than a raw sum. Prefer a maintained reference implementation for Core Web Vitals, then add route and release context and report at visibility changes as well as unload.

```ts caption="Observe a specific entry type and forward only the diagnostic data you need."
new PerformanceObserver((list) => {
  for (const entry of list.getEntries()) {
    sendDiagnostic(entry.entryType, entry.startTime, entry.duration);
  }
}).observe({ type: "longtask", buffered: true });
```
